"2026-11-15T07:29:34+00:00"}},{"id":"codegen-quality-ci","name":"Code generation and quality tooling","kind":"decision","summary":"Generated files (static imports, async client, CLI reference, types) are verified by make style/quality, ruff, ty and mypy, with split CI test suites.","decision":"Generated artifacts are checked in and enforced by utils scripts and CI; tests split into api/transfer/inference suites.","alternatives":"Runtime generation; manual maintenance.","why":"Keeps the public surface and docs in sync.","paths":["utils","Makefile","pyproject.toml",".github/workflows","tests/conftest.py","CLAUDE.md"],"centrality":3,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.571,"history":[{"sha":"be0e648","date":"2022-09-12","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Lucain","subject":"Add autocomplete + tests + type checking (#1041)"},{"sha":"33f5f5f","date":"2023-10-30","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Lucain","subject":"remove black formatter to use only ruff (#1783)"},{"sha":"8c9546f","date":"2026-09-18","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Lucain","subject":"[Quality] Replace `re.match` with `re.fullmatch` / `re.search` and ban it via ruff (#4939)"},{"sha":"7da19ff","date":"2026-10-01","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Lucain","subject":"[CI] Split tests into api / transfer / inference suites (#5042)"},{"sha":"174626d","date":"2026-06-29","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Lucain","subject":"[Tests] Migrate test_hf_api.py from unittest to pytest (#4407)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Keeps generated files and code quality consistent. Formatting, linting, type checks and CI test suites all run through it.","decision":"Generated outputs (static imports, async client, CLI docs, types) are committed and checked by utils scripts through make style and make quality and by CI. Runtime generation and manual upkeep were not used. The aim is a public surface and docs that stay in sync. Tests are split into api, transfer and inference suites.","where":[{"path":"utils","what":"generator and checker scripts"},{"path":"Makefile","what":"style and quality targets"},{"path":".github/workflows","what":"CI workflows"}],"shaped_by":"Lucain wrote the 2022–2023 tooling alone [human]; his 2026 quality, test-migration and CI-split work was [assisted].","basis":"4ac7719b8648","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063","generated_at":"2026-11-15T07:29:34+00:00"}},{"id":"mcp-agent","name":"MCP and Tiny Agents","kind":"module","summary":"MCPClient and agent loop that connect InferenceClient to MCP tool servers, with a CLI.","decision":"Agent built on the async InferenceClient and the mcp package as an optional extra.","alternatives":"Dependence on external agent frameworks.","why":"Python parity with Tiny Agents in JS.","paths":["src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp"],"centrality":2,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.262,"history":[{"sha":"aee73c4","date":"2025-05-21","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"célina","subject":"[MCP] Tiny Agents in Python (#3098)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Lets an LLM served through InferenceClient call tools on MCP servers. It includes a small agent loop and a CLI.","decision":"The agent is built on the async InferenceClient and the optional mcp package. External agent frameworks were not used. The stated aim is parity with Tiny Agents in JS.","where":[{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp","what":"MCPClient, agent loop and CLI"}],"shaped_by":"célina wrote it in May 2025 [human].","basis":"2353e232d4e7","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063","generated_at":"2026-11-15T07:29:34+00:00"}},{"id":"sandbox","name":"Sandbox and jobs","kind":"module","summary":"Client for Hub sandboxes with a pooled, context-bound cache, plus jobs API.","decision":"Pool cache bound to the context that wrote it; sandbox is a library module with CLI wrapper.","alternatives":"No caching; remote-only handles.","why":"A fix to prevent cross-context reuse; otherwise unclear.","paths":["src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox.py","src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","src/huggingface_hub/_jobs_api.py"],"centrality":2,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.262,"history":[{"sha":"40efc5e","date":"2026-09-14","klass":"assisted","agent":"claude-code","author":"Lucain","subject":"fix(sandbox): bind the pool cache to the context that wrote it (#4838)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"A client for creating and talking to Hub sandboxes, with a local cache of pooled sandbox handles. Jobs API types live alongside.","decision":"The pool cache is bound to the context that wrote it, so another context cannot reuse it. Sandbox is a library module with a CLI wrapper on top. No caching and remote-only handles were not chosen. The history shows this was a fix, and other reasons are unclear.","where":[{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox.py","what":"sandbox client"},{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","what":"pooled cache"},{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/_jobs_api.py","what":"jobs API types"}],"shaped_by":"Lucain made the cache-binding fix in Sept 2026 [assisted] with Claude Code.","basis":"21813188ba19","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063","generated_at":"2026-11-15T07:29:34+00:00"}},{"id":"webhooks","name":"Webhooks server and payloads","kind":"module","summary":"Gradio/FastAPI-based webhook receiver and typed payload definitions.","decision":"Optional server built on Gradio/FastAPI with pydantic payloads.","alternatives":"Documented raw HTTP only.","why":"Easy local webhook handling in Spaces.","paths":["src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_payload.py"],"centrality":2,"scope":"architectural","importance":0.247,"history":[{"sha":"25e20ef","date":"2023-04-14","klass":"human","agent":null,"author":"Lucain","subject":"[Feat] Webhook server (#1410)"}],"parent":null,"orientation":{"purpose":"Lets a user run a small local server to receive Hub webhook events. It provides typed payload definitions for them.","decision":"An optional server built on Gradio and FastAPI, with pydantic payload models. Documenting raw HTTP only was not chosen. The reason given is easy webhook handling in Spaces.","where":[{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","what":"webhook server"},{"path":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_payload.py","what":"payload models"}],"shaped_by":"Lucain wrote it in April 2023 [human].","basis":"60dc8626e074","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063","generated_at":"2026-11-15T07:29:34+00:00"}}],"connections":[["cli","commit-upload","uses",3.0],["cli","download-cache","uses",4.0],["cli","hf-api","uses",28.5],["cli","hf-filesystem-buckets","uses",5.0],["cli","http-auth-config","uses",38.0],["cli","inference","uses",1.0],["cli","repocard-mixin","uses",4.0],["cli","sandbox","uses",2.5],["codegen-quality-ci","cli","uses",2.0],["codegen-quality-ci","hf-api","uses",9.0],["codegen-quality-ci","http-auth-config","uses",3.0],["codegen-quality-ci","inference","uses",2.0],["commit-upload","download-cache","uses",4.0],["commit-upload","hf-api","uses",5.0],["commit-upload","http-auth-config","uses",5.0],["download-cache","hf-api","uses",6.0],["download-cache","http-auth-config","uses",10.0],["hf-api","cli","uses",1.0],["hf-api","commit-upload","uses",2.0],["hf-api","download-cache","uses",3.0],["hf-api","hf-filesystem-buckets","uses",2.0],["hf-api","http-auth-config","uses",7.5],["hf-api","inference","uses",5.0],["hf-api","mcp-agent","uses",2.0],["hf-api","repocard-mixin","uses",7.0],["hf-api","sandbox","uses",1.5],["hf-api","webhooks","uses",2.0],["hf-filesystem-buckets","commit-upload","uses",1.0],["hf-filesystem-buckets","download-cache","uses",4.0],["hf-filesystem-buckets","hf-api","uses",6.0],["hf-filesystem-buckets","http-auth-config","uses",6.0],["http-auth-config","commit-upload","uses",1.0],["http-auth-config","hf-api","uses",4.0],["inference","hf-api","uses",16.0],["inference","http-auth-config","uses",8.0],["mcp-agent","hf-api","uses",3.0],["mcp-agent","http-auth-config","uses",1.0],["mcp-agent","inference","uses",4.0],["repocard-mixin","download-cache","uses",2.0],["repocard-mixin","hf-api","uses",8.0],["repocard-mixin","http-auth-config","uses",7.0],["sandbox","hf-api","uses",4.0],["sandbox","http-auth-config","uses",3.5],["cli","codegen-quality-ci","together",9.7],["cli","commit-upload","together",0.38],["cli","download-cache","together",5.03],["cli","hf-api","together",57.33],["cli","hf-filesystem-buckets","together",3.22],["cli","http-auth-config","together",12.58],["cli","inference","together",0.31],["cli","mcp-agent","together",0.41],["cli","repocard-mixin","together",0.58],["cli","sandbox","together",9.73],["cli","webhooks","together",0.13],["codegen-quality-ci","commit-upload","together",0.92],["codegen-quality-ci","download-cache","together",7.52],["codegen-quality-ci","hf-api","together",23.04],["codegen-quality-ci","hf-filesystem-buckets","together",1.16],["codegen-quality-ci","http-auth-config","together",8.0],["codegen-quality-ci","inference","together",30.36],["codegen-quality-ci","mcp-agent","together",0.2],["codegen-quality-ci","repocard-mixin","together",9.87],["codegen-quality-ci","sandbox","together",0.5],["codegen-quality-ci","webhooks","together",0.85],["commit-upload","download-cache","together",10.47],["commit-upload","hf-api","together",25.84],["commit-upload","hf-filesystem-buckets","together",1.97],["commit-upload","http-auth-config","together",6.39],["commit-upload","inference","together",0.29],["commit-upload","repocard-mixin","together",4.05],["commit-upload","webhooks","together",0.51],["download-cache","hf-api","together",32.66],["download-cache","hf-filesystem-buckets","together",2.12],["download-cache","http-auth-config","together",21.72],["download-cache","inference","together",1.5],["download-cache","repocard-mixin","together",10.66],["download-cache","sandbox","together",0.17],["download-cache","webhooks","together",0.27],["hf-api","hf-filesystem-buckets","together",10.54],["hf-api","http-auth-config","together",39.3],["hf-api","inference","together",19.16],["hf-api","mcp-agent","together",1.58],["hf-api","repocard-mixin","together",25.57],["hf-api","sandbox","together",11.16],["hf-api","webhooks","together",1.9],["hf-filesystem-buckets","http-auth-config","together",1.84],["hf-filesystem-buckets","inference","together",0.68],["hf-filesystem-buckets","mcp-agent","together",0.6],["hf-filesystem-buckets","repocard-mixin","together",1.37],["hf-filesystem-buckets","sandbox","together",0.46],["hf-filesystem-buckets","webhooks","together",0.13],["http-auth-config","inference","together",3.94],["http-auth-config","repocard-mixin","together",6.75],["http-auth-config","sandbox","together",0.8],["http-auth-config","webhooks","together",0.64],["inference","mcp-agent","together",2.32],["inference","repocard-mixin","together",1.33],["inference","webhooks","together",0.41],["mcp-agent","repocard-mixin","together",0.4],["repocard-mixin","webhooks","together",1.06]],"hubs":[],"order":["commit-upload","http-auth-config","download-cache","inference","repocard-mixin","hf-filesystem-buckets","sandbox","cli","mcp-agent","webhooks","hf-api","codegen-quality-ci"],"tour":{"markdown":"# Architecture tour: huggingface_hub\n\nWritten by `udebt tour` at `2c61ae8c` on 2026-11-15; 12 units, about 11 minutes to read. Units come in the order to learn them: each after the units it uses.\n\nThis repository is the Python client for the Hugging Face Hub: it lets code and terminal users create repos, move files in and out, run inference, and manage Hub resources. The HfApi client is the central entry point that exposes nearly every Hub operation. It sits on the HTTP, auth and configuration core, and delegates heavy lifting to the Commit and upload pipeline and the Download and cache layer. Around them sit higher-level surfaces: HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload, Repo cards and ModelHubMixin, the Inference client and providers, MCP and Tiny Agents, Sandbox and jobs, and Webhooks server and payloads. The hf CLI wraps most of these for humans and agents, while Code generation and quality tooling keeps the generated files and public surface consistent.\n\n## The map\n\n*Uses*: its code imports the other unit's (strongest first). *Used by*: how many units import it. *Changes with*: the units commits most often touch with it.\n\n1. **Commit and upload pipeline** (`commit-upload`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`, `download-cache`; used by 4; changes with `hf-api`, `download-cache`\n2. **HTTP, auth and configuration core** (`http-auth-config`): uses `hf-api`, `commit-upload`; used by 10; changes with `hf-api`, `download-cache`\n3. **Download and cache layer** (`download-cache`): uses `http-auth-config`, `hf-api`; used by 5; changes with `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`\n4. **Inference client and providers** (`inference`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`; used by 4; changes with `codegen-quality-ci`, `hf-api`\n5. **Repo cards and ModelHubMixin** (`repocard-mixin`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`, `download-cache`; used by 2; changes with `hf-api`, `download-cache`\n6. **HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload** (`hf-filesystem-buckets`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`, `download-cache`, `commit-upload`; used by 2; changes with `hf-api`, `cli`\n7. **Sandbox and jobs** (`sandbox`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`; used by 2; changes with `hf-api`, `cli`\n8. **hf CLI** (`cli`): uses `http-auth-config`, `hf-api`, `hf-filesystem-buckets`, `download-cache`, `repocard-mixin`, `commit-upload`, `sandbox`, `inference`; used by 2; changes with `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`\n9. **MCP and Tiny Agents** (`mcp-agent`): uses `inference`, `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`; used by 1; changes with `inference`, `hf-api`\n10. **Webhooks server and payloads** (`webhooks`): used by 1; changes with `hf-api`, `repocard-mixin`\n11. **HfApi client** (`hf-api`): uses `http-auth-config`, `repocard-mixin`, `inference`, `download-cache`, `commit-upload`, `hf-filesystem-buckets`, `mcp-agent`, `webhooks`, `sandbox`, `cli`; used by 10; changes with `cli`, `http-auth-config`\n12. **Code generation and quality tooling** (`codegen-quality-ci`): uses `hf-api`, `http-auth-config`, `cli`, `inference`; changes with `inference`, `hf-api`\n\n## 1. Commit and upload pipeline (`commit-upload`)\n\nThis unit turns a list of add, delete and copy operations into a single Hub commit, and decides how each file travels: as a plain payload, through LFS, or through Xet. It uses the HfApi client, the HTTP, auth and configuration core, and the Download and cache layer. It is used by the hf CLI, the HfApi client, HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload, and the core; it tends to change together with the HfApi client, the cache layer and the core. The decision: stream the commit as newline-delimited JSON after classifying files up front, and push big folders through a streamed multi-commit pipeline on hf_xet. Git pushes, one-shot multipart commits and a separate large-folder API were passed over, because streaming allows many files per commit and Xet gives chunk-level deduplication and resumability. Lucain wrote the streaming in 2022 [human]; the Xet pipeline, which supersedes the older large-folder path, came in 2026 with Claude Code assistance [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/_commit_api.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_upload_pipeline.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/lfs.py`.\n\n## 2. HTTP, auth and configuration core (`http-auth-config`)\n\nThis is the shared foundation for all network access: creating HTTP clients with retries and backoff, building request headers, finding and storing tokens, login flows, error types, input validation, and every environment-driven setting. It sits at the bottom of the stack, used by nearly everything else, including the hf CLI, the cache layer, inference, repo cards, filesystem and buckets, the commit pipeline, sandbox, MCP and tooling. It also lists the HfApi client and commit pipeline as things it uses, and changes alongside the HfApi client, the cache layer and the CLI. The decision: one place for retry, headers and configuration, rather than each module owning an HTTP client, so retry, error mapping and user-agent behavior stay uniform. Keyring-only auth and config files were not chosen. Celina Hanouti added multiple local tokens in 2024, and célina added browser-based OAuth login in 2026, both alone [human]. The history does not show deeper reasons.\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/utils/_http.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_login.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/constants.py`.\n\n## 3. Download and cache layer (`download-cache`)\n\nThis unit downloads single files or whole repositories and keeps them in a local cache, so revisions are shared, offline use works and repeat downloads are cheap. It uses the HTTP, auth and configuration core and the HfApi client. It is used by the hf CLI, the commit pipeline, HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload, the HfApi client, and Repo cards and ModelHubMixin, and it changes with the HfApi client, the core and the repo-card unit. The decision: a git-like cache of content-addressed blobs with snapshot links pointing at them, a fallback for systems lacking symlinks, and a metadata mode for local folders. Flat caches, git clones and an external cache service were rejected; downstream libraries rely on this layout. Julien Chaumond and Lucain designed it in 2022 [human]. Lucain and célina added the on-disk tree listing cache and a cross-repo shared blob store in 2026 with Claude Code help [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/file_download.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_snapshot_download.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_tree_cache.py`.\n\n## 4. Inference client and providers (`inference`)\n\nThis unit lets users run machine-learning tasks such as chat, image generation and embeddings against Hugging Face and third-party services through one interface. It uses the HfApi client and the HTTP, auth and configuration core. It is used by the HfApi client, MCP and Tiny Agents, Code generation and quality tooling, and the hf CLI, and it changes together with the tooling, the HfApi client and the core. The decision: keep the synchronous client hand-written as the source of truth, generate the asynchronous twin from it, and give each provider a small adapter that maps the shared task interface onto that provider's HTTP API. Separate SDKs per provider and a hand-written async client were avoided, since one interface across twenty-odd providers is the goal and generation keeps sync and async in step. The history provided does not show who shaped it or when.\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/inference/_client.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/inference/_providers`, `utils/generate_async_inference_client.py`.\n\n## 5. Repo cards and ModelHubMixin (`repocard-mixin`)\n\nThis unit reads, edits and pushes model and dataset cards, meaning the README with its metadata header, and gives machine-learning framework classes ready-made save, push and load behavior. It uses the HfApi client, the HTTP, auth and configuration core, and the Download and cache layer. It is used by the HfApi client and the hf CLI, and changes with the HfApi client, the cache layer and the tooling unit. The decision: cards are YAML front matter plus templates, and the mixin lets each framework integration share one implementation, with safetensors as the default weight format for PyTorch. Framework-specific integrations and pickle-based weights were rejected, for easier downstream adoption and for safety. Leandro von Werra, Lucain and Benjamin Muskalla made the key calls between 2022 and 2024, including the safetensors default in early 2024, all as human work [human].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/repocard.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/templates`.\n\n## 6. HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload (`hf-filesystem-buckets`)\n\nThis unit gives Hub repos and buckets a file-system interface through fsspec, so tools like pandas and datasets can read them as if they were folders. It also holds live-following of bucket changes and hot reload for Spaces. It uses the HfApi client, the HTTP, auth and configuration core, the Download and cache layer and the Commit and upload pipeline. It is used by the hf CLI and the HfApi client, and changes with the HfApi client, the CLI and the cache layer. The decision: POSIX-like access through fsspec, with buckets as a distinct Xet-backed storage type. Offering only download and upload helpers, or inventing a custom file API, was passed over to gain interoperability with the data ecosystem. Charles wrote hot reload in February 2026 [human]; Lucain's buckets work that same month was done with Cursor [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/hf_file_system.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_buckets.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_hot_reload`.\n\n## 7. Sandbox and jobs (`sandbox`)\n\nThis unit is a client for creating and talking to Hub sandboxes, with a local cache of pooled sandbox handles, alongside the types for the jobs API. It uses the HfApi client and the HTTP, auth and configuration core. It is used by the hf CLI and the HfApi client, and changes with the HfApi client, the CLI and the core. The decision: the pooled cache is tied to the context that wrote it, so a different context cannot reuse its entries, and sandbox behavior lives in a library module with the command line as a thin wrapper. Having no cache, or only remote handles, were the alternatives not taken. The history shows this was a bug fix by Lucain in September 2026, made with Claude Code [assisted]; it does not show why caching or pooling was chosen originally.\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_jobs_api.py`.\n\n## 8. hf CLI (`cli`)\n\nThis is the hf command line, giving terminal users, scripts and AI agents access to Hub operations with consistent output. It uses the core, the HfApi client, filesystem and buckets, the cache layer, repo cards, the commit pipeline, sandbox and inference. It is used by the tooling unit, which generates its reference docs, and by the HfApi client, and changes with the HfApi client, the core and sandbox. The decision: Typer command groups registered in one entry point, all sharing a single output object that supports auto, JSON, human, quiet and agent formats, with centralized error handling. Raw argparse or click, one script per command and plain printing were avoided for consistent machine- and human-readable output; the repository's written conventions prescribe this. célina and Lucain wrote the 2026 commands and renames [human]; later migrations and additions by célina and Caleb Fahlgren used Cursor [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/cli/hf.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/cli/_output.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/cli/_cli_utils.py`.\n\n## 9. MCP and Tiny Agents (`mcp-agent`)\n\nThis unit lets a language model served through the inference client call tools exposed by MCP servers. It contains a small agent loop and a command line. It uses the Inference client and providers, the HfApi client, and the HTTP, auth and configuration core. The HfApi client uses it, and it changes with the inference unit, the HfApi client, and HfFileSystem, buckets and hot reload. The decision: build the agent directly on the asynchronous inference client and the optional mcp package. Depending on external agent frameworks was avoided; the stated reason is parity with Tiny Agents in JavaScript. célina wrote it in May 2025 [human]. The history provided gives no further rationale beyond that parity goal.\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp`.\n\n## 10. Webhooks server and payloads (`webhooks`)\n\nThis unit lets a user run a small local server that receives Hub webhook events, with typed definitions of what those events contain. It is used by the HfApi client, and it changes together with the HfApi client, Repo cards and ModelHubMixin, and Code generation and quality tooling. It lists no units that it uses. The decision: an optional server built on Gradio and FastAPI, with pydantic models for the payloads. Simply documenting the raw HTTP format was not chosen; the purpose, as far as the record shows, is making webhook handling easy to set up locally and in Spaces. Lucain wrote it alone in April 2023 [human]. The history does not explain in more detail why Gradio and FastAPI were picked over other approaches.\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_payload.py`.\n\n## 11. HfApi client (`hf-api`)\n\nThis is the main Python entry point to the Hub's REST API: repos, files, discussions, Spaces, collections, webhooks and more. It is used by the CLI, inference, tooling, repo cards, cache layer, filesystem and buckets, commit pipeline, core, sandbox and MCP, and it in turn uses most of those units. It changes with the hf CLI, the core and the cache layer. The decision: one large class, with module-level functions forwarding to its methods and a generated lazy-import package entry point, returning dataclasses. Per-resource clients, a generated OpenAPI client and pydantic responses were not chosen; the history points to backward compatibility for downstream libraries, and dataclasses replaced an older repr helper for lightweight typed results. Mario Šaško, Lucain, Freddy Boulton and Michael shaped it from 2022 to 2024 [human]; Lucain's 2026 endpoints migration was Claude Code assisted [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/__init__.py`, `src/huggingface_hub/_space_api.py`.\n\n## 12. Code generation and quality tooling (`codegen-quality-ci`)\n\nThis unit keeps generated files and code quality consistent: formatting, linting, type checks and the continuous-integration test suites. It uses the HfApi client, the core, the hf CLI and the inference unit, since it generates or verifies their outputs. It changes with the inference unit, the HfApi client and Repo cards and ModelHubMixin. The decision: generated outputs such as static imports, the async client, CLI reference docs and types are committed, then checked by scripts through the style and quality commands and in CI. Runtime generation and manual upkeep were not used, so the public surface and docs stay in sync. Tests are split into api, transfer and inference suites. Lucain wrote the 2022 to 2023 tooling alone [human]; his 2026 quality, test-migration and CI-split work used Claude Code [assisted].\n\nOpen first: `utils`, `Makefile`, `.github/workflows`.\n\n---\n\nNext: `udebt path` for the order to learn these in and the time each takes, `udebt dig <unit>` for a walkthrough of one with its code.\n","minutes":11},"walkthroughs":{"hf-api":{"flow":"A user calls `from huggingface_hub import create_repo; create_repo(\"me/my-space\", repo_type=\"space\", space_sdk=\"gradio\")` to create a new Space on the Hub.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/__init__.py","start_line":1226,"end_line":1259,"narration":"`create_repo` is not imported when the package loads. The module-level `__getattr__` looks the name up in a table generated from `_SUBMOD_ATTRS` (a script maintains it, so never edit it by hand) and imports `hf_api` on first use. This is how the package exposes hundreds of names cheaply from one flat namespace."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py","start_line":15510,"end_line":15551,"narration":"The `hf_api` module builds one default `HfApi()` instance, `api`. Each public function is just an alias for that instance's bound method (`create_repo = api.create_repo`). So the user's call runs a method on a shared default client, and anything the client holds (token, endpoint, headers) is shared by all module-level calls. Adding an `HfApi` method does not make it importable until it is aliased here and exported in `__init__`."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py","start_line":4666,"end_line":4704,"narration":"The method (wrapped in `@validate_hf_hub_args` at line 4581) first splits `repo_id` into namespace and name, then validates locally: repo type, visibility resolution, and the optional Space template lookup, which makes a nested call to another `HfApi` method. Most Hub rules, such as the SDK being required for Spaces, are enforced client-side in this one class. They can drift from what the server accepts, and they are why the class is so large."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py","start_line":4726,"end_line":4753,"narration":"Here the typed Python arguments become the JSON payload. Space-only arguments are copied in only for Spaces, and otherwise they only trigger a warning. Optional fields are added only when set, so the REST schema lives implicitly in this code instead of in a generated client or pydantic model."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py","start_line":11565,"end_line":11585,"narration":"The next step, in the POST at 4755-4757, builds headers through this helper. It uses the client's own token and user-agent settings unless the call passes a `token`. `token=None` means 'use the client default', while `False` means 'send no auth'. That is why the code tests `is None` rather than using `or`. This is what an `HfApi` instance contributes over the bare functions."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_api.py","start_line":4755,"end_line":4791,"narration":"The POST runs in a loop. A 409 with a specific concurrency message is retried forever, and `hf_raise_for_status` then converts other failures into typed exceptions. With `exist_ok=True`, a 409 is swallowed, and a 401/402/403 triggers a follow-up `repo_info` call to check whether the repo exists. Success returns a `RepoUrl`, a `str` subclass that parses `repo_id` and `repo_type` out of the URL."}],"trap":"A reader may think the module-level `create_repo` is a separate implementation, or that `HfApi` instances are independent of it. It is just the bound method of one shared `api = HfApi()` created at import, so all behaviour lives in the class, and the public name only exists once the alias and the generated `__init__` entry both exist.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"download-cache":{"flow":"A user calls snapshot_download(\"some/repo\", revision=\"main\") for a repo with Xet-backed files, so the download runs end to end and fills the cache.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_snapshot_download.py","start_line":277,"end_line":298,"narration":"The branch name is resolved to a commit hash with one repo_info call. The recursive tree listing for that commit is then read from disk, or fetched and written as a JSON file under the repo's cache folder. The listing is keyed by commit hash, so it never goes stale. Later per-file downloads can reuse it. Lucain added this in 2026 with AI assistance (#4394). A dry run does not write the listing."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/file_download.py","start_line":1905,"end_line":1929,"narration":"Each file download is handed that tree cache folder. For Xet-tracked files it rebuilds what a HEAD request would return: the ETag is the LFS sha256, the size is the LFS size, and the Xet hash and refresh route come from the listing. This saves one round trip per file. Anything missing, such as no listing, no Xet hash or non-Xet files, returns None and the normal HEAD call runs. This is the cache's fast path; the ETag it produces names the blob."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/file_download.py","start_line":1224,"end_line":1242,"narration":"Once the commit hash and ETag are known, an existing snapshot pointer ends the call early. The ref update is best-effort, so a read-only cache still works. Otherwise the blobs/ and snapshots/ folders are created. For a branch or tag name, refs/<revision> is written to hold the commit hash, so offline use can map the name to a snapshot. This is the git-like layout downstream libraries depend on."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/file_download.py","start_line":1273,"end_line":1297,"narration":"The lock is per repo and ETag, and it only reduces duplicate downloads. Correctness comes from downloading to a temporary file and renaming it. Under the lock, the code first tries to link the blob in from the cross-repo shared store (célina, 2026, #4498), keyed by Xet hash. Only if that fails does it download. A fresh, Xet-verified download is then published to the shared store. Shared blobs are used only when symlinks work, because the blob itself becomes a symlink."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/file_download.py","start_line":700,"end_line":724,"narration":"The last step is the snapshot pointer. By default it is a relative symlink into blobs/, so a moved cache stays valid, and several revisions share one blob. If the platform has no symlinks, the same call degrades to real files. A new blob is moved into place. An existing blob is copied, because it may still be referenced elsewhere. This is the no-symlink fallback from the design decision, and it gives up the deduplication."}],"trap":"A reader may think the cache folder holds the real files per revision and that the tree listing is a convenience cache that can go stale. In fact the real bytes live once in blobs/, named by ETag, and the snapshots are only symlinks (or copies where symlinks fail). The tree listing is keyed by immutable commit hash, so it cannot go stale, and it can stand in for the HEAD request.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"http-auth-config":{"flow":"A user with a browser-OAuth login calls an HfApi method (e.g. model_info) with no explicit token, so headers are built from the stored token and the request goes out through http_backoff.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_headers.py","start_line":107,"end_line":122,"narration":"Every Hub call starts by asking build_hf_headers for headers. It resolves a token, builds the user-agent, adds `authorization: Bearer ...` only if a token exists, and lets caller-supplied headers override. Because all network code goes through this one function, user-agent and auth behave the same everywhere, which is the point of centralizing."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_headers.py","start_line":125,"end_line":154,"narration":"Token resolution is three-valued: a string is used as-is, False sends nothing, True requires a stored token and raises if none exists. For the implicit None case, the HF_HUB_DISABLE_IMPLICIT_TOKEN constant is checked before get_token() on purpose. get_token() can refresh an OAuth token (a network call plus file writes), and that must not happen if the result would be discarded."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_auth.py","start_line":81,"end_line":86,"narration":"The precedence chain is OIDC exchange, then HF_TOKEN env var, then the token file, then Google Colab secrets. Env therefore beats a `hf auth login` token on disk. The file step is the refreshing variant, which is where the 2026 browser-OAuth work (célina) hooks into the older multi-token store from Celina Hanouti's 2024 commit."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_auth.py","start_line":255,"end_line":330,"narration":"The refresh logic looks up the active token in the multi-token store to find its refresh_token and expires_at. A token with no refresh metadata (a plain `hf_...` token) is cached and returned unchanged. If expiry is near, it takes a cross-process file lock, re-reads the store, and either adopts another process's refresh or exchanges the refresh token and saves the result. Any failure is swallowed: it returns the old token and sets a recheck time (never, for invalid_grant), so auth trouble shows up as a server 401 rather than an exception here."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_http.py","start_line":499,"end_line":566,"narration":"With headers built, the call reaches the retry loop shared by http_backoff and http_stream_backoff. Each attempt fetches the shared client with get_session(), because a ConnectError closes the shared client. Retried status codes honour rate-limit/Retry-After headers. Other failures use exponential backoff capped at max_wait_time. After max_retries, hf_raise_for_status turns the response into a typed Hub error."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/utils/_http.py","start_line":283,"end_line":317,"narration":"This request event hook runs on every request made by the default client. It blocks the request in offline mode, tags download calls, and adds a request ID (the X-Amzn-Trace-Id header) for server-side debugging. The debug log records only whether an authorization header was present, not its value. Per-module clients would each have to repeat this, so it is the main payoff of the single client factory."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the stored token file is always what gets sent, but get_token() checks OIDC and the HF_TOKEN env var first, and an OAuth token can be silently refreshed (or fail to refresh without raising) at the moment headers are built.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"commit-upload":{"flow":"A user calls upload_folder on a large local directory with Xet enabled, and the streamed multi-commit pipeline turns its add operations into one or more Hub commits.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_upload_pipeline.py","start_line":447,"end_line":467,"narration":"`run` is the entry point. It starts a daemon committer thread and runs the coordinator on the caller's thread, so scanning and uploading overlap with committing. On any exception it sets an abort event and aborts the Xet session. On a clean finish it sends a sentinel and joins the committer. This two-thread design is what makes the multi-commit pipeline streamed."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_commit_api.py","start_line":762,"end_line":779,"narration":"This is the pre-upload classification step, and the coordinator calls it once per chunk of files. For each file it POSTs the path, size and a small content sample to the Hub's preupload endpoint. The Hub answers with an upload mode (regular, LFS or Xet), a gitignore verdict and the remote oid. The results are written back onto the operations, so everything downstream branches on these fields. Lucain's 2022 NDJSON work introduced this classify-first design; the 2026 pipeline reuses it."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_upload_pipeline.py","start_line":506,"end_line":530,"narration":"Ignored files are dropped. Regular files only add to a byte budget, because their content travels inline in the commit. Any other mode opens a Xet upload-commit for the batch, and `start_upload_*` begins sending bytes in the background immediately. The sha256 is computed by hf_xet while it chunks, unless it is already known. So Xet uploading is not a separate phase after classification; it begins as soon as each chunk is classified."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_upload_pipeline.py","start_line":531,"end_line":553,"narration":"A batch is cut when it reaches the pacer's target file count, the regular-bytes budget, or the time limit. It is then put on a bounded queue, where a full queue blocks the coordinator. That blocking is the backpressure: scanning and Xet uploading cannot run far ahead of commits. This is what splits one `upload_folder` call into several commits."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_upload_pipeline.py","start_line":582,"end_line":610,"narration":"The committer thread handles each batch in order. It first waits for the Xet upload-commit to finalize, because a git commit may only reference Xet files after that. It then reads back any sha256 that hf_xet computed. Next it drops files whose remote oid equals the local oid, which prevents empty commits. Only the remaining files reach the git commit. Reordering these steps would break commits that reference files not yet uploaded."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_commit_api.py","start_line":1053,"end_line":1068,"narration":"`_send_commit` is shared with the classic `create_commit` path. It serializes the payload as newline-delimited JSON with the `application/x-ndjson` content type and POSTs it to the commit endpoint. The pipeline passes `retry_on_error=True`, which uses backoff. The docstring warns that a lost response on a successful commit would then produce a duplicate commit."}],"trap":"A reader may assume upload_folder uploads every file and then makes a single commit, with LFS/Xet uploads happening after the files are classified; in fact the pipeline classifies files in chunks, starts Xet uploads while scanning continues, and makes several commits (part N) whose size adapts to failures.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"cli":{"flow":"A user runs `hf models ls --author google --json --limit 3` in a terminal and expects a JSON array of models.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/hf.py","start_line":130,"end_line":150,"narration":"At import time hf.py registers every command and group as a lazy placeholder, not the real implementation. Only the names and (module, attribute) pairs are recorded, so `hf --help` and shell completion stay fast without importing the huge Hub client. Adding a new top-level group means adding a row to `_LAZY_GROUPS` here."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/_cli_utils.py","start_line":154,"end_line":169,"narration":"When click dispatches `models`, the custom group's resolver imports the real module and swaps it into `self.commands`. It then hands off to click's normal resolution. This is click underneath, so `typer_factory` returns a custom click group (the `Option`/`Argument` types come from `_framework.py`), not real Typer. That is why every group inherits this hook."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/_cli_utils.py","start_line":192,"end_line":198,"narration":"Once click resolves a leaf (`ls`), the resolver calls the flag pre-processor on the remaining args before click parses them. Only leaves are processed, because group resolution is recursive and `models` is resolved first. This is the single choke point where formatting becomes global, so individual commands never declare `--format`."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/_cli_utils.py","start_line":509,"end_line":523,"narration":"`--json` and `-q` are deleted from the args list, and the chosen mode is applied to the `out` singleton. Conflicting flags raise a usage error here. `models_ls` therefore never sees `--json`, and with no flag the mode stays `auto`, which `set_mode` resolves to agent or human by environment detection. Commands that define their own `--format` (such as `hf jobs ls`) are diverted earlier and skip this stripping."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/models.py","start_line":190,"end_line":213,"narration":"The command body only builds an `HfApi` (via `get_hf_api`), converts each result to a dict and calls `out.table(results)`. It contains no formatting logic, so the same call produces a padded table, TSV, JSON or id-only lines depending on the singleton's mode. The trailing `out.hint` is a follow-up suggestion following the CLAUDE.md convention."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/cli/hf.py","start_line":165,"end_line":179,"narration":"If `list_models` raises, for example a RepositoryNotFoundError or a gated or HTTP error, the exception reaches `main()`. `format_known_exception` maps it to a friendly message, `out.error` prints it, and the process exits 1 with a hint about HF_DEBUG. Unknown exceptions are re-raised with their full traceback. That central handling is why commands should not wrap API calls in try/except."}],"trap":"A reader will assume this is a Typer app where each command parses its own `--format`/`--json` flag; in fact it is a custom click framework that strips those flags from the args before parsing and sets a global `out` singleton, so commands that declare their own `--format` have to opt out.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"inference":{"flow":"A user calls InferenceClient(provider=\"auto\").chat_completion(messages, model=\"org/model\") and gets a ChatCompletionOutput back (or a stream of chunks with stream=True).","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_client.py","start_line":882,"end_line":895,"narration":"chat_completion first settles which model string drives routing and which goes into the payload: the client-level model wins for the URL, the per-call model wins for the body. It then asks the provider registry for a helper for the task name 'conversational'. Every task method follows this same shape, so the client holds no provider-specific knowledge."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_providers/__init__.py","start_line":228,"end_line":263,"narration":"The registry resolves provider and task to one helper instance. A URL model forces hf-inference. 'auto' on a conversational task returns the server-side auto-router with no lookup. Otherwise 'auto' picks the first provider mapped to the model. Unknown providers or unsupported tasks raise ValueError here, before any network call to the inference service."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_client.py","start_line":897,"end_line":926,"narration":"The client packs the unified OpenAI-style arguments into a flat parameters dict, merges in extra_body, and hands everything to the helper's prepare_request. It gets back a ready-made request description and performs the actual HTTP call through _inner_post. The client never builds URLs, headers or payloads itself."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_providers/_common.py","start_line":88,"end_line":124,"narration":"This is the template method every adapter inherits. Each step (api key, model mapping, headers, URL, payload) is a separate hook, so a provider overrides only what differs. The output is a RequestParameters with either a JSON payload or raw bytes, never both."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_providers/_common.py","start_line":269,"end_line":289,"narration":"For chat, the base conversational adapter targets the OpenAI-style /v1/chat/completions route. It replaces the user's model with the provider-mapped id and drops None values. Placing 'model' first in the body is deliberate, so a router can read it from a small prefix of the request. Providers with different chat APIs subclass and override these hooks."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_client.py","start_line":253,"end_line":278,"narration":"_inner_post is the one place that performs the HTTP request. It streams the body, returns line iterators when stream=True, and converts httpx timeouts into InferenceTimeoutError. This method and the call sites are what the async generator rewrites, which keeps the sync file as the single source of truth."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the async client is a separately maintained implementation, but it is regenerated from the sync client's source text by utils/generate_async_inference_client.py, and make style and make quality fail if it drifts, so edits belong in _client.py, never in _async_client.py.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"hf-filesystem-buckets":{"flow":"A user opens `HfFileSystem(live_follow=True)` and calls `ls(\"buckets/user/my-bucket/logs\")`. Another process then uploads a file, and the user lists the folder again and sees the new file without passing `refresh=True`.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_file_system.py","start_line":549,"end_line":556,"narration":"Every call starts by resolving the string path. The `buckets/` prefix makes the parsed URI a bucket, so the filesystem checks the bucket exists and returns a bucket-specific resolved path. Repos and buckets share one fsspec surface, but from here on they take different branches. Lucain's Buckets API commit (Feb 2026, assisted with Cursor) is where this branch was added."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_file_system.py","start_line":857,"end_line":864,"narration":"`_ls_tree` is the choke point where live-follow plugs in. For a bucket path with `live_follow` on, it subscribes to the bucket's follower. It then drains the `_pending_bucket_changes` queue before looking at `dircache`. A `None` entry means 'reconcile, drop the whole bucket tree'. A real batch becomes targeted invalidation. Draining here, on the caller's thread, keeps `dircache` single-writer."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_file_system.py","start_line":679,"end_line":702,"narration":"A follower thread is shared by every filesystem instance with the same endpoint, token and bucket. Subscribers are held in a weak-value dict, so the thread dies once all instances are garbage collected (`is_alive`). The `push` callback fans each batch out to every subscriber's queue. If a subscriber hasn't listed for 10,000 pending entries, it is dropped and re-joins fresh on its next listing. This bounds memory for idle instances. The follower is created and started under a global lock, and the new subscriber's bucket listings are invalidated."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_bucket_live_follow.py","start_line":367,"end_line":380,"narration":"The follower thread's loop starts with no resume point. It reads the bucket's `updatedAt` and calls `on_reconcile`, which pushes `None` so consumers drop their cached view. The server then replays anything newer than that instant, which closes the race between the first listing and the subscription. If the server doesn't serve the event feed, the thread logs a warning and exits. The filesystem then silently works as an ordinary cached one."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_bucket_live_follow.py","start_line":418,"end_line":438,"narration":"One session consumes the server-sent event stream. The `changes` batches go to `on_changes`, which is the `push` above, and the cursor is saved as the resume point. A `reset` clears the resume point so the loop reconciles again. A `reconnect` keeps the cursor. During replay, `changes` arrive before `ready`, so the cache can be corrected before the follower reports itself subscribed."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hf_file_system.py","start_line":651,"end_line":666,"narration":"On the next `ls`, queued changes are applied as invalidation, not patching. For each changed path the code drops the cached listing of the path's subtree and of every ancestor up to the bucket root. The next listing re-fetches only those directories from the Hub. This is why the user sees the new file: `logs` is no longer in `dircache`, so `_ls_tree` falls through to the API call."}],"trap":"A reader may assume live-follow pushes changes straight into the filesystem's cached listings in the background. In fact the follower thread only queues events, and the cache is mutated by the owning instance during its next `_ls_tree` call, as invalidation rather than an in-place update.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"repocard-mixin":{"flow":"A user calls push_to_hub(\"my-awesome-model\") on a PyTorch model class that inherits PyTorchModelHubMixin, which writes weights, config and a generated README card to a temp folder and uploads it.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py","start_line":185,"end_line":244,"narration":"The flow starts earlier, when the user's class is defined. __init_subclass__ runs at class-creation time and builds a MixinInfo holding the card template and a ModelCardData. It copies the parent's info and layers on the keyword metadata (tags, library_name, repo_url and so on). This is how a framework integrator customises cards with no code beyond class keywords."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py","start_line":661,"end_line":678,"narration":"push_to_hub creates the repo with exist_ok, then calls save_pretrained into a SoftTemporaryDirectory and uploads that folder in one commit with upload_folder. Pushing is just save-then-upload, so the local save format is exactly what lands on the Hub. The temp directory means nothing is left on disk afterwards."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py","start_line":402,"end_line":428,"narration":"save_pretrained is the template method that fixes the order. It deletes any stale config.json, calls the framework hook _save_pretrained, writes the config only if the hook did not already write one, then writes README.md only if none exists. The 'do not overwrite' guards let subclasses and users override either file. The same method is the local-save path, and it calls push_to_hub again when push_to_hub=True."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py","start_line":744,"end_line":749,"narration":"This is the PyTorch hook. It writes weights with safetensors' save_model to the single-file safetensors name. It unwraps .module for DataParallel models. Benjamin Muskalla's 2024-02 change (#2033) made this the default in place of pickle, so uploaded weights cannot execute code when loaded."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/hub_mixin.py","start_line":680,"end_line":689,"narration":"generate_model_card feeds the class-level MixinInfo (card data, template string, repo, paper and docs URLs) plus any per-call kwargs into ModelCard.from_template. This is where the class metadata from the first step becomes card content."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/repocard.py","start_line":324,"end_line":333,"narration":"from_template merges the card data's dict with the template kwargs, and the explicit kwargs win. It renders the jinja template with the YAML front-matter produced by card_data.to_yaml(), then parses the result back into a RepoCard. Jinja is an optional dependency, so this raises ImportError without it. save() then writes the string to README.md."}],"trap":"A reader may assume that PyTorchModelHubMixin only handles safetensors files, or that save_pretrained always regenerates the README. In fact loading falls back to a pickle file (read with weights_only) when no safetensors file exists, and an existing README.md or config.json in the save directory is never overwritten.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"codegen-quality-ci":{"flow":"A contributor edits InferenceClient, runs make style to regenerate checked-in files, and then pushes a PR whose CI re-checks those files and runs the tests as api, transfer and inference suites.","steps":[{"file":"Makefile","start_line":7,"end_line":24,"narration":"`quality` and `style` run the same scripts. `quality` only checks and exits non-zero on drift. `style` passes `--update` so each generator rewrites its file. The generated artifacts are committed, so this pair is what keeps them in sync with their sources."},{"file":"utils/check_static_imports.py","start_line":105,"end_line":134,"narration":"This is the pattern every checker follows. It regenerates the expected `__init__.py` content from the lazy-import definitions and compares it with the file on disk. With `update` it overwrites the file. Without it, it prints a hint to run `make style` and returns False. Editing a facade by hand therefore fails the check."},{"file":"utils/generate_async_inference_client.py","start_line":74,"end_line":108,"narration":"The async client is derived from the sync `InferenceClient` source by text transforms, formatted, and compared with the committed `_async_client.py`. A mismatch exits 1 and names the likely cause, which is a sync change without a regeneration. With `--update` it rewrites the file and exits 0. This is why the generated file is never edited by hand."},{"file":".github/workflows/python-quality.yml","start_line":42,"end_line":57,"narration":"CI repeats the checks without `--update`, so a stale generated file fails the PR. It also adds the inference-types generator and the task-parameter check, which the local `quality` target omits. It runs `uvx ty check src` and mypy as well. Lucain moved to ruff alone in 2023 and added the CI-only type checkers over time."},{"file":"tests/conftest.py","start_line":52,"end_line":78,"narration":"Lucain added the api / transfer / inference split in 2026 with claude-code assistance. This hook gives each test exactly one suite marker. `xet` and `no_xet` imply `transfer`, and unmarked tests default to `api`. A test with two suites is flagged invalid, and the failure is raised at setup rather than at collection."},{"file":".github/workflows/python-tests.yml","start_line":55,"end_line":134,"narration":"The CI matrix names the suites defined in conftest and selects them with `-m`. Transfer runs twice, with and without hf_xet, using the expression `transfer and not xet`. If a new test lacks a marker it falls into `api`, so the suite definitions in conftest and the matrix must stay in step."}],"trap":"A reader may assume `make quality` is the full CI gate, but CI also runs the inference-type and task-parameter checks, `uvx ty` at the latest version and mypy, which the local target does not run.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"mcp-agent":{"flow":"A user runs the Tiny Agents CLI with an agent config, types a prompt, and the agent calls a tool on an MCP server before answering.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/cli.py","start_line":33,"end_line":65,"narration":"The CLI starts by loading the agent config (servers, inputs, prompt) and installing a two-stage Ctrl+C handler. The first press sets an abort event that the agent loop polls. The second sets an exit event to quit. The abort path is cooperative, so it only takes effect between turns, not inside a running tool call."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/agent.py","start_line":37,"end_line":55,"narration":"Agent subclasses MCPClient and adds only a system-prompt message list and the stored server configs. load_tools just feeds each config to add_mcp_server. This thin layer is why célina's design reuses one async client plus the optional mcp package instead of an external agent framework."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/mcp_client.py","start_line":75,"end_line":112,"narration":"MCPClient builds an AsyncInferenceClient, which is how an MCP-aware agent reaches any provider. If a base_url is given, the model name is kept only for the payload. An AsyncExitStack owns every server connection, so exiting the context closes them all together."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/mcp_client.py","start_line":199,"end_line":250,"narration":"After a transport is opened (stdio, SSE or HTTP), a session is initialised and its tools are listed, following pagination cursors. An optional allowed_tools filter is applied. Each tool is recorded in the sessions map by name (first server wins on duplicates) and converted into a chat-completion function-tool definition with the MCP input schema as its parameters."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/agent.py","start_line":72,"end_line":100,"narration":"run appends the user message, then loops one model turn at a time via process_single_turn_with_tools. It stops when an exit tool was called, when the turn cap is exceeded, or when the model answers in plain text without calling tools. Otherwise a tool result is the last message and the loop goes again so the model can read it. The abort event is checked at the top of each iteration."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/inference/_mcp/mcp_client.py","start_line":348,"end_line":399,"narration":"After streaming, each accumulated tool call is parsed and run on the session registered for that name. Bad JSON, a missing session or a raised exception become a tool message with an error string. Those errors are fed back to the model and do not raise. Exit-tool calls end the turn with an empty tool message."}],"trap":"It is easy to assume a failing MCP tool or malformed model output crashes the agent. Those failures are caught per tool call and returned to the model as tool-message text so the loop continues.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"sandbox":{"flow":"A user calls SandboxPool.connect(\"my-pool\", namespace=\"org-B\") in a new process, after another process previously ran `pool create` for that pool, and then calls create() to get a sandbox.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","start_line":74,"end_line":109,"narration":"The decision lives here. A CacheContext is endpoint + credential fingerprint + namespace, and its key is a digest that names the cache subdirectory. The token is hashed locally (no whoami call), so a hit costs zero round-trips, while a rotated or different token simply maps to a different directory and misses. Lucain's Sept 2026 fix (assisted with Claude Code) made this the binding between an entry and who wrote it."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox.py","start_line":1526,"end_line":1543,"narration":"connect() builds a context from the caller's own api and the namespace argument, then asks the cache. It does not read the namespace out of the file. A hit rebuilds the pool with no HTTP and `_connect_mode=True`. A miss falls through to the cold path, which finds a host by labels through the Jobs API."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","start_line":161,"end_line":190,"narration":"The read side has two layers. The path already includes the context key, so a foreign context never finds its file. Every failure (missing, corrupt, wrong version, mistyped, foreign) returns None and never raises, so a bad cache costs latency and not an error in the middle of create()."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","start_line":304,"end_line":323,"narration":"Second layer of the binding: the file also records its own context, endpoint, namespace and pool id, and these are compared to the caller's. This catches files that were moved or copied into a directory they were not written for. A cross-namespace hit would be a confidentiality problem, because the fast path sends the HF bearer to the cached host URL."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox.py","start_line":2211,"end_line":2243,"narration":"On the first create(), cached hosts are seeded from the file under the pool's context. A cached URL is checked for plausibility first. Entries older than HOST_TRUST_TTL (15 minutes) are re-checked with inspect_job before they get credentials. Fresh entries are trusted as they are, which keeps `pool create` followed by `create --pool` free of extra requests. Hosts are marked unverified and dropped on the first failed request."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_sandbox_cache.py","start_line":205,"end_line":238,"narration":"After the pool learns about hosts, it persists them. This merges under a file lock: it reads the existing hosts, upserts the new ones, removes dead ones, then writes atomically. So concurrent processes add to the file and never drop each other's hosts. All errors are swallowed, so a failed write never breaks sandbox creation."}],"trap":"A reader may think the cache is a record of the pool that connect() trusts and copies its settings from, but the namespace and credential come only from the caller and the file is trusted only if it matches them, so a mismatch is a silent miss that falls back to label discovery.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"},"webhooks":{"flow":"A user decorates an async function with `@webhook_endpoint`, runs the script, and the Hub then POSTs a repo-update event with an `x-webhook-secret` header to `/webhooks/<name>`.","steps":[{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","start_line":275,"end_line":296,"narration":"The decorator fetches a process-wide `WebhooksServer`, registers the function, and on the first registration schedules `app.launch` with `atexit`. That is why a plain script starts serving at the end of the file, and why `func.launch` exists for notebooks. Nothing is mounted on a web server yet."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","start_line":99,"end_line":151,"narration":"The constructor reads the secret from the argument or `WEBHOOK_SECRET`, and warns if none is set. `add_webhook` only stores the function in a dict under `/webhooks/<path>` and rejects duplicates. This is a registry, not a FastAPI route, which is what lets Gradio own the real server later."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","start_line":153,"end_line":173,"narration":"`launch` starts the Gradio UI non-blocking and takes the FastAPI app Gradio creates. Only then does it attach each registered function with `fastapi_app.post`, wrapping it with the secret check when a secret exists. This is the core reason for the Gradio/FastAPI decision (Lucain, 2023): Spaces already serve a Gradio app, so webhook routes ride on that server. Sharing is on by default when run locally, so the Hub can reach it."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_server.py","start_line":333,"end_line":378,"narration":"For each incoming POST the wrapper runs first. A missing header gives 401 and a wrong value gives 403, both before user code runs. Otherwise it calls the user function, sync or async. It also rewrites the function's signature to add `request`, because FastAPI builds its parameter injection from the signature. A middleware would be cleaner, but cannot be added once Gradio has started the app."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_payload.py","start_line":112,"end_line":133,"narration":"When the user function has a `WebhookPayload` annotation, FastAPI parses the JSON body into these pydantic models, and a malformed body is rejected with a 422 before user code runs. Fields such as `event`, `repo`, and the optional `discussion` and `comment` mirror the Hub's version 3 report format."},{"file":"src/huggingface_hub/_webhooks_payload.py","start_line":21,"end_line":32,"narration":"Pydantic is optional. Without it a dummy `BaseModel` is defined, so importing the module works and the `ImportError` appears only when a payload is instantiated. The server and payload modules therefore both degrade lazily instead of breaking `import huggingface_hub`."}],"trap":"A reader may assume the webhook routes are defined when `add_webhook` or the decorator is called, and that the secret is checked by FastAPI middleware. In fact routes are attached only inside `launch`, after Gradio has started the app, and the secret check is a per-route wrapper that exists only when a secret is set.","head":"2c61ae8c11eedb1b79e262dd9eb5e68962cac063"}}}$u7d981bbd$::jsonb,
    null);
commit;
